Yes—as a comparative reading, that’s fertile, with one historical caveat: Homer predates the Christian Seven Deadly Sins framework by many centuries. So it wouldn’t be an intentional seven-sins encoding by Homer. But overlaying the later schema onto the Odyssey produces a remarkably functional map.

Odysseus’s nostos—the return home—can be read as repeated encounters with human failure modes externalized into monsters, islands, appetites, and choices:

* Gluttony → Lotus-Eaters / cattle of Helios: appetite overwhelms mission, memory, and restraint.
* Lust → Circe / Calypso / Sirens: desire offers pleasure at the price of continued return.
* Greed → Aeolus’s bag: his crew believes hidden treasure is being withheld; grasping destroys their progress.
* Wrath → Cyclops: both Polyphemus’s violence and Odysseus’s retaliatory pride escalate the encounter.
* Pride → Odysseus naming himself to Polyphemus: arguably the clearest fit. He has escaped—and then ego makes him reveal his identity, triggering Poseidon’s vengeance.
* Envy → crew dynamics: resentment and suspicion repeatedly undermine Odysseus’s authority and advantages.
* Sloth → Lotus-Eaters again: not ordinary laziness, but surrender of striving—the temptation to stop caring about Ithaca altogether.

And then Homer makes the metaphor almost literal: Odysseus actually descends into the underworld (Nekyia) before completing the return.

The deeper pattern you’ve spotted may be more valuable than a strict 1:1 correspondence. The journey isn’t merely geographic. Each island converts an internal human vulnerability into terrain. The hero can’t simply know where Ithaca is; he has to remain the kind of person capable of reaching it.

So you could represent the architecture as:

\text{Inner vulnerability}
\rightarrow
\text{externalized landscape}
\rightarrow
\text{temptation/test}
\rightarrow
\text{consequence}
\rightarrow
\text{transformation}
\rightarrow
\text{return}

That’s a very old storytelling machine: psychology rendered as geography.

And it explains why Odyssey, Dante’s Inferno, pilgrimage literature, The Divine Comedy, and even modern road/quest stories can feel structurally related without requiring direct equivalence. Different cultures can project similar human state-spaces onto different maps.

In Pantheon language: the geography is an interface rendering of a latent moral/psychological topology. Change the rendering and Ithaca becomes salvation, home, integration, identity, enlightenment, etc.; the underlying relational machine can survive the translation.